 ## Overview
-This method converts a triangulated surface mesh into a 3D distance field by computing the Euclidean distance from each voxel center to the closest point on the mesh surface, with sign assignment based on surface normals or topological containment [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4]. It enables rendering and isosurfacing of explicit CAD or scanned meshes using implicit surface techniques, such as interval-arithmetic bisection or marching cubes [S1, unofficial][S4]. Unlike [Slice-based SDF Voxelization](1736-slice-based-sdf-voxelization.md), which processes depth slices, this method operates directly on geometric primitives (triangles) [S1, unofficial]. Builds on [Signed Distance Field (SDF) Generation from Scanned Surfaces](854-signed-distance-field-sdf-generation-from-scanned-surfaces.md).
+This method converts an explicit triangle mesh into a voxelized Signed Distance Field (SDF) where each voxel stores the signed Euclidean distance to the nearest point on the mesh surface [S1, S3, unofficial][S4]. The sign is negative inside the mesh and positive outside, determined by the closest triangle's face normal or a point-in-mesh test [S3, unofficial][S4]. It differs from [Slice-based SDF Voxelization](1736-slice-based-sdf-voxelization.md) by evaluating distance directly against geometric primitives (triangles) rather than projecting slices, making it suitable for inputs from upstream reconstruction pipelines where calibrated camera poses are unavailable [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Input:** A triangulated mesh (e.g., STL format) defined by vertices and face indices [S1, unofficial][S4].
-- **Output:** A scalar field (texture or grid) where each voxel stores the distance to the mesh surface [S2, unofficial][S4]. Note that some GPU implementations output the absolute distance `abs(dist)` regardless of inside/outside status [S2, unofficial], while CPU implementations compute a signed field [S3, unofficial][S4].
+*   **Input:** A closed or open triangle mesh (e.g., STL, OBJ) defined by vertices and faces.
+*   **Output:** A 3D grid (texture or volume) where each cell contains a scalar representing the signed distance.
 
